---
// Each job in the usual stack, and what does it on substrate. Products are named only where the
// caveat below keeps the comparison fair.
const jobs = [
  {
    job: 'database',
    usual: 'Postgres',
    here: 'Your own PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector. Substrate keeps a table per declared type.'
  },
  {
    job: 'api layer',
    usual: 'PostgREST, Hasura',
    here: 'Every declared type can be queried and mutated over HTTP and MCP, and subscribed to over frame streams, with no handler code. Policies decide who may do what.'
  },
  {
    job: 'vector store',
    usual: 'a separate vector database',
    here: 'Rows of a type that declares it are embedded on write into pgvector, in the same Postgres. Recall fuses dense and keyword ranking.'
  },
  {
    job: 'agent memory',
    usual: 'Mem0, Zep',
    here: "Memory is more types: rows your agents write, query and recall by meaning, in the same store as the data they're about."
  },
  {
    job: 'coordination',
    usual: 'queues and glue code',
    here: 'Each agent signs with its own key, subscribes to the rows it cares about, and every row records which key wrote it.'
  }
];
---
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="replaces-title">
  <div class="joint signal" aria-hidden="true"><span class="pulse"></span></div>
  <p class="label kicker">// what it replaces</p>
  <h2 id="replaces-title">Five jobs in the usual stack. One server on your own Postgres.</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    A database, an API in front of it, a vector store beside it, a memory service for your agents,
    and the glue that lets them hand each other work. Substrate does each job from one process, in
    one frame grammar.
  </p>
  <ol class="jobs">
    {
      jobs.map((j) => (
        <li data-reveal>
          <p class="label job">
            {j.job}
            <span>{j.usual}</span>
          </p>
          <p class="here">{j.here}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p class="caveat">
    It is not a drop-in for any of them. Substrate serves the types you declare, not an existing
    schema, and recall ranks what your agents wrote rather than having a model extract memories
    from their conversations.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .jobs {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin: 40px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px 40px;
    padding: 22px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  li p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .job {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .job span {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* The job and its products on the left, substrate's answer in the wider right column. */
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;
    }
  }

  .caveat {
    max-width: 40em;
    margin-top: 28px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 17px;
  }
</style>
